WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.090 --> 00:00:03.100
- The first thing I thought,
as a singer having to do videos

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:06.480
all the time and having to
time things with the lyrics,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.370
when you did the entrance and the song,

00:00:09.370 --> 00:00:11.043
I was like how many times
did you have to do that?

00:00:11.043 --> 00:00:14.430
- I think 27 was the
official take on the day.

00:00:14.430 --> 00:00:15.593
Because Fred
- Was that like

00:00:15.593 --> 00:00:17.020
one of the hardest ones?
- Fred had nine years,

00:00:17.020 --> 00:00:18.420
or 14 years in order to figure this out.

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:19.690
It was nearly impossible.

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:21.656
You think that song lasts a long time.

00:00:21.656 --> 00:00:22.489
- It's not.
- That song

00:00:22.489 --> 00:00:25.070
does not last a long time when
you gotta carry a thing in,

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.220
take off a jacket, open closet door,

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.490
pull a sweater off a hanger

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:29.323
- I had a mini panic attack
- Put the jacket

00:00:29.323 --> 00:00:30.690
on the hanger.
- Watching it.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:35.330
- Maury, the boss, Maury Heller,
our director, she confessed

00:00:35.330 --> 00:00:38.330
to me later on that she
blew it, that she could've

00:00:38.330 --> 00:00:41.420
made it easier, but then
she found out that Fred's

00:00:41.420 --> 00:00:45.240
blue shoes were already halfway tied.

00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:46.960
They were bigger than his feet anyway,

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.730
so they went on really easy.

00:00:48.730 --> 00:00:51.300
Then the shoes that come off
are also bigger than my feet,

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:52.250
so those come off easy.

00:00:52.250 --> 00:00:54.750
But I had to do the full tie.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:56.996
You know, criss-cross applesauce.

00:00:56.996 --> 00:00:58.080
- Yeah, I watched.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:00.402
- A rabbit goes around
the ear and in the hole.

00:01:00.402 --> 00:01:01.235
- Yes.
- I had to do

00:01:01.235 --> 00:01:02.068
that whole thing.

00:01:02.068 --> 00:01:04.130
He only had to do the rabbit
around, the fly around

00:01:04.130 --> 00:01:04.963
- Oh my Gosh.

00:01:04.963 --> 00:01:07.193
- It was a nightmare.

00:01:08.430 --> 00:01:10.430
- What's the nicest thing,
'cause we have a whole

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:11.650
neighbor thing we do on our show too.

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:13.860
The whole reason why I
signed on to do this show,

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:15.670
is 'cause I feel like
we're really divided.

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.000
So I wanted my show to kind
of connect people and actually

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:19.330
meet your neighbors instead
of being on your phone

00:01:19.330 --> 00:01:21.469
all the time and actually be present.

00:01:21.469 --> 00:01:24.240
I was curious, have you
ever had a really cool

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:25.681
neighbor moment in your life?

00:01:25.681 --> 00:01:28.930
- My dad was divorced and he was working

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.170
in the restaurant business,
so there were three kids that

00:01:31.170 --> 00:01:34.400
were living in an apartment
complex in Alameda, California.

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.170
My dad would leave us food to prepare,

00:01:37.170 --> 00:01:40.340
frozen peas and vegetables that
we would literally just take

00:01:40.340 --> 00:01:43.840
to the garbage chute, and
frozen we'd just throw it away,

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.200
'cause we're not makin' those peas.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:48.463
So we had really, really
super bad eating habits.

00:01:50.078 --> 00:01:51.030
A lady moved over.

00:01:51.030 --> 00:01:52.810
She had three kids.

00:01:52.810 --> 00:01:54.800
One of them was, I only remember one name.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:56.070
His name was Marco.

00:01:56.070 --> 00:01:59.600
But she lived just like two
doors down on the opposite side

00:01:59.600 --> 00:02:00.824
of the hall.

00:02:00.824 --> 00:02:03.230
Her door was always open a crack,

00:02:03.230 --> 00:02:05.960
because we could always
just go in and out.

00:02:05.960 --> 00:02:07.870
She would always make us food.

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.790
Corn dogs, the first time you have

00:02:09.790 --> 00:02:11.246
a corn dog made with love.
- I'm Texan.

00:02:11.246 --> 00:02:13.471
Tom, would you like a,
yes, what is a corn dog?

00:02:13.471 --> 00:02:15.030
It was magnificent.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.930
- Every kid kinda has
something they latch onto

00:02:16.930 --> 00:02:18.810
when they're younger, and
I love that Mr. Rogers

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:20.280
pulled that out of Lloyd.

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:21.680
Because Matthew Reece's
character played Lloyd,

00:02:21.680 --> 00:02:23.680
and he had 10, the original 10.

00:02:23.680 --> 00:02:25.810
- We compared notes on all of this.

00:02:25.810 --> 00:02:28.080
I don't remember having
anything that was given to me,

00:02:28.080 --> 00:02:32.010
but I remember buying that thing
that I had my heart set on.

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.450
Major Matt Mason, the posable, bendable

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:36.529
astronaut doll by Mattel.

00:02:36.529 --> 00:02:38.300
- Major Matt Mason.
- Major Matt Mason.

00:02:38.300 --> 00:02:39.866
This was during,
- So specific.

00:02:39.866 --> 00:02:43.009
- You know, men were landin'
on the moon, and it cost $1.95.

00:02:43.009 --> 00:02:44.190
I had a paper dollar.

00:02:44.190 --> 00:02:45.210
I had some nickels.

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.240
I had a couple quarters.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.320
- It was all your money.

00:02:47.320 --> 00:02:50.700
- I saved up $1.95, went
to Clark's Drugstore

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.100
on my brother's borrowed
bicycle, picked it off the rack

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:55.290
- We had the same childhood.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:57.070
- It's in the plastic
shell and it's on a rack.

00:02:57.070 --> 00:02:59.550
You gotta kinda work it
loose, then you take it up,

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.750
and the shelf is here and
the lady, I put it down

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:03.400
and I put out all my coins.

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.130
And she says, all right, very good.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.820
And with sales tax that will be $2.06.

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.770
- Oh, that's sad.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.496
- I was 11 cents short.

00:03:11.496 --> 00:03:12.374
- [Kelly] Did she spot you?

00:03:12.374 --> 00:03:13.303
- No.

00:03:13.303 --> 00:03:14.520
- That is wrong, that is
not a neighborly thing.

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:15.610
- Goof life lesson.

00:03:15.610 --> 00:03:18.910
Son, you're close, go out
and earn another 11 cents.

00:03:18.910 --> 00:03:23.110
And I went home and went through
the cushions of the couch.

00:03:23.110 --> 00:03:24.420
Lift up the tank of the toilet,

00:03:24.420 --> 00:03:25.253
there might be somethin' in there.

00:03:25.253 --> 00:03:26.380
- We did that too.

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:27.870
In the movie, people recognize you.

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.400
I love this part of the
movie, when they recognize you

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.813
and all the kids start singing.

00:03:30.813 --> 00:03:31.827
- Yeah, that really happened.

00:03:31.827 --> 00:03:33.810
- So has that ever happened to you?

00:03:33.810 --> 00:03:36.990
- When Big came out, my
dad and my step-mom were

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.290
in New York, and we went to
a really fancy restaurant.

00:03:39.290 --> 00:03:42.420
There was a pianist and
a harpist and a violinist

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.570
playing a little music.

00:03:44.774 --> 00:03:47.570
The piano dance in Big
was the heart and soul.

00:03:47.570 --> 00:03:49.790
Well, suddenly a piano
and a harp and a violinist

00:03:49.790 --> 00:03:51.710
started playing Heart and Soul.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:52.723
- Oh my gosh.

00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:56.580
- I think this is because of the movie.

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:58.150
- Do you get embarrassed
in that situation,

00:03:58.150 --> 00:03:59.160
or do you think it's cool?

00:03:59.160 --> 00:04:01.390
- It's better than the
other stuff they yell at ya.

00:04:01.390 --> 00:04:02.414
- Yeah, exactly.

00:04:02.414 --> 00:04:03.885
- Wilson, you know.

00:04:03.885 --> 00:04:05.074
I get a lot of that.

00:04:05.074 --> 00:04:06.220
Brace for impact.

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:08.750
Anything that landed in a movie.

00:04:08.750 --> 00:04:11.500
- We actually call our balls
Wilson at our house. (laughs)

00:04:11.500 --> 00:04:12.501
- [Tom] There ya go.

00:04:12.501 --> 00:04:13.890
- We go, we're like grab
Wilson, put him in the car.

00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:16.628
- Get a little bit of every,
yeah son, you have a problem.

00:04:16.628 --> 00:04:18.720
(laughs)

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.130
- How did playing Mr. Rogers
change you as a person?

00:04:21.130 --> 00:04:23.590
I'm asking you this because I
grew up watching Mr. Rogers,

00:04:23.590 --> 00:04:25.740
and it changed me as a
child and helped me develop,

00:04:25.740 --> 00:04:28.300
but at the same time,
watching it as an adult,

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:29.320
because I've seen the documentary

00:04:29.320 --> 00:04:30.690
and I've also seen the film.

00:04:30.690 --> 00:04:31.981
- The documentary's a beautiful thing.

00:04:31.981 --> 00:04:33.210
- It is, and it does change you.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.920
It makes you re-think your
actions with your children,

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:37.400
especially as an adult.

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.420
- Every role that comes
out enlightens you somehow,

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.110
because you have to ponder
something about what the

00:04:42.110 --> 00:04:44.970
character goes through and how
it relates to your own life.

00:04:44.970 --> 00:04:47.490
There hasn't been a time I
haven't walked away from a job

00:04:47.490 --> 00:04:49.940
thinking like, oh man, I
gotta re-think that aspect

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:52.210
of my personality defects.
- They all change you.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:56.110
- Mr. Rogers was all about
listening to invest in what

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:57.520
the other person is saying.

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:02.160
Since then, I have made a study
of how I listen to people.

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:04.610
There's this acronym, W-A-I-T.

00:05:04.610 --> 00:05:06.750
That stands for why am I talking.

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:09.520
There have been many times where
I've been in a circumstance

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:12.553
where I have told myself to
shut up and start listening.

00:05:13.670 --> 00:05:17.611
It's been an extraordinary,
I've learned so much about

00:05:17.611 --> 00:05:20.120
my kids by listening to them more

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.750
than asking them questions.

00:05:21.750 --> 00:05:22.583
- You have to work at it.

00:05:22.583 --> 00:05:25.193
- The concept of listening
is a human endeavor.

00:05:26.575 --> 00:05:30.860
There's been so many times
where, contrary to my nature

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.900
of being so glib and loud,

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:37.330
I have been working on listening better.

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:40.530
- Matthew and Susan were
both telling us that you gave

00:05:40.530 --> 00:05:42.445
the most awesome wrap gift.

00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:43.860
They were like, it's so beautiful.

00:05:43.860 --> 00:05:44.693
It's leather.

00:05:44.693 --> 00:05:46.210
It's inscribed.

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.250
It's like so beautiful.
- Got their name on it.

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:49.979
- Yes, and they were
like, I just don't know

00:05:49.979 --> 00:05:51.141
what it is.
- What it is, yeah.

00:05:51.141 --> 00:05:52.910
(laughs)

00:05:52.910 --> 00:05:55.120
- And you're Tom Hanks, so
they were too afraid to ask.

00:05:55.120 --> 00:05:56.339
- Here's what it is.

00:05:56.339 --> 00:05:59.990
When you show up to work
on a movie, you have a copy

00:05:59.990 --> 00:06:02.007
of the pages that you're,
they're called sides.

00:06:02.007 --> 00:06:03.100
- Sides, yeah.

00:06:03.100 --> 00:06:04.880
- The script pages that
you're shooting that day

00:06:04.880 --> 00:06:05.713
and the call sheets.

00:06:05.713 --> 00:06:08.170
So everybody knows
exactly what you're doing.

00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:10.880
They are leather holders for those sides.

00:06:10.880 --> 00:06:13.530
And this little pen and
you can keep notes on it.

00:06:13.530 --> 00:06:18.100
I've used it on complicated
days, 'cause you need to know.

00:06:18.100 --> 00:06:22.140
And the fact that
neither Susan nor Matthew

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:23.080
knew what they were.

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:23.913
- No, but the fact

00:06:23.913 --> 00:06:25.124
that they were two
- Are these people

00:06:25.124 --> 00:06:25.957
professional actors?

00:06:25.957 --> 00:06:28.350
- I think they were
like, it's so beautiful.

00:06:28.350 --> 00:06:31.260
She even said, I don't know,
some sort of saddle. (laughs)

00:06:31.260 --> 00:06:32.920
I don't know what she said.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:33.753
- Use it as a coin purse.

00:06:33.753 --> 00:06:34.980
I don't care.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:37.270
- But it was funny though
because then they're gonna

00:06:37.270 --> 00:06:38.760
figure it out though and
it makes perfect sense.

00:06:38.760 --> 00:06:39.860
It's the most perfect wrap gift.

00:06:39.860 --> 00:06:40.980
- It's a tool.
- It's beautiful.

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:42.210
Now they know.
- Now they know.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:43.043
- The more you know.

00:06:43.043 --> 00:06:45.220
- Yeah, so take it down from the shelf,

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.940
take it out of its shrink
wrap, and use the damn things

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:49.730
for cryin' out loud.

00:06:49.730 --> 00:06:50.920
- I wanna say thank you so much.

00:06:50.920 --> 00:06:52.330
For a kid that grew up on Mr. Rogers,

00:06:52.330 --> 00:06:53.764
you did such a great job.

00:06:53.764 --> 00:06:56.790
Like anybody had any doubt, but it's just,

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:58.230
that's an important role
for a lot of people,

00:06:58.230 --> 00:06:59.410
and you did such a great job.

00:06:59.410 --> 00:07:00.317
- Well thanks.

